\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

\subsection{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:setup}

We evaluate OpenVLA-7B~\cite{kim2024_openvla,karamcheti2024} on LIBERO-Goal~\cite{liu2023_libero} (10 tasks, 50 demonstrations each).
Representations are extracted from six sources spanning the VLA pipeline (\S\ref{sec:repr_extraction}): DinoSigLIP+Projector, LLM image\_mean and readout tokens at each of 32 layers, DINOv2~\cite{oquab2024_dinov2} (\texttt{cls\_token}/\texttt{patch\_mean}), CLIP~\cite{radford2021_clip} (\texttt{patch\_mean}), and branch-split (DINOv2 1{,}024-D / SigLIP 1{,}152-D before fusion).
Three LLM conditions are compared: VLA-trained, pretrained Llama-2 base (no VLA fine-tuning), and untrained (6.74B parameters reinitialized to random weights), crossed with instruction presence (\S\ref{sec:model_data}).
RSA computes Spearman $\rho$ between cosine-distance representation RDMs and Euclidean-distance action RDMs, with 10{,}000 bootstrap CIs (\S\ref{sec:rsa}).
MLP probes (input $\to$ 256 $\to$ 7; Adam, lr${}=10^{-3}$, 50 epochs, early stopping) report mean $R^2$ (\S\ref{sec:probes}).
This yields $N = 63{,}228$ observations (after excluding terminal frames without next-action labels; $63{,}728$ total); RSA uses task-level blocking ($N_\text{eff} = 10$) and probes use demo-level train/test splits.
All patterns reproduce with a linear probe (Appendix~\ref{sec:linear_probe}).

%------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Framework Validation: Token-Level Specialization}
\label{sec:token_specialization}

As a sanity check on the experimental framework, we train cross-instruction probes (\S\ref{sec:cross_instruction_probes}): given the same visual frame with each of 10 task instructions, we classify which instruction was present from per-layer activations.

\paragraph{Image tokens are instruction-blind (expected).}
As predicted by the causal mask and frozen encoder, image\_mean instruction classification remains at chance ($10\%$) across all 32 layers in all conditions (Table~\ref{tab:dense_routing_main}).
Both linear and MLP probes corroborate this, as does standalone DINOv2 ($10\%$; Appendix~\ref{sec:instruction_invariance_appendix}).

\paragraph{Readout token as multimodal hub.}
Trained readout achieves $100\%$ 10-way classification from L1 through L31 (LOOCV-confirmed; Appendix~\ref{sec:loocv}); untrained achieves $100\%$ at L1 but decays to $82\%$ by L31.
The readout is thus the primary locus of multimodal integration, with richer task-relevant content than standalone DINOv2 (QA probe $86.3\%$ vs.\ $82.7\%$; Appendix~\ref{sec:qa_probe}), replicating on LIBERO-Spatial (Appendix~\ref{sec:cross_benchmark_appendix}).

