\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

\subsection{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:setup}

We evaluate OpenVLA-7B~\cite{kim2024_openvla,karamcheti2024} on LIBERO-Goal~\cite{liu2023_libero} (10 tasks, 50 demonstrations each).
Representations are extracted from six sources spanning the VLA pipeline (\S\ref{sec:repr_extraction}): DinoSigLIP+Projector, LLM image\_mean and readout tokens at each of 32 layers, DINOv2~\cite{oquab2024_dinov2} (\texttt{cls\_token}/\texttt{patch\_mean}), CLIP~\cite{radford2021_clip} (\texttt{patch\_mean}), and branch-split (DINOv2 1{,}024-D / SigLIP 1{,}152-D before fusion).
Three LLM conditions are compared: VLA-trained, pretrained Llama-2 base (no VLA fine-tuning), and untrained (6.74B parameters reinitialized to random weights), crossed with instruction presence (\S\ref{sec:model_data}).
RSA computes Spearman $\rho$ between cosine-distance representation RDMs and Euclidean-distance action RDMs, with 10{,}000 bootstrap CIs (\S\ref{sec:rsa}).
MLP probes (input $\to$ 256 $\to$ 7; Adam, lr${}=10^{-3}$, 50 epochs, early stopping) report mean $R^2$ (\S\ref{sec:probes}).
This yields $N = 63{,}228$ observations (after excluding terminal frames without next-action labels; $63{,}728$ total); RSA uses task-level blocking ($N_\text{eff} = 10$) and probes use demo-level train/test splits.
All patterns reproduce with a linear probe (Appendix~\ref{sec:linear_probe}).

%------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Framework Validation: Token-Level Specialization}
\label{sec:token_specialization}

As a sanity check on the experimental framework, we train cross-instruction probes (\S\ref{sec:cross_instruction_probes}): given the same visual frame with each of 10 task instructions, we classify which instruction was present from per-layer activations.

\paragraph{Image tokens are instruction-blind (expected).}
As predicted by the causal mask and frozen encoder, image\_mean instruction classification remains at chance ($10\%$) across all 32 layers in all conditions (Table~\ref{tab:dense_routing_main}).
Both linear and MLP probes corroborate this, as does standalone DINOv2 ($10\%$; Appendix~\ref{sec:instruction_invariance_appendix}).

\paragraph{Readout token as multimodal hub.}
Trained readout achieves $100\%$ 10-way classification from L1 through L31 (LOOCV-confirmed; Appendix~\ref{sec:loocv}); untrained achieves $100\%$ at L1 but decays to $82\%$ by L31.
The readout is thus the primary locus of multimodal integration, with richer task-relevant content than standalone DINOv2 (QA probe $86.3\%$ vs.\ $82.7\%$; Appendix~\ref{sec:qa_probe}), replicating on LIBERO-Spatial (Appendix~\ref{sec:cross_benchmark_appendix}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Cross-instruction 10-way classification at selected layers (full profile in Appendix~\ref{sec:dense_routing}). Image\_mean remains at chance ($10\%$) in all conditions; trained readout reaches $100\%$ at L1 and holds; untrained readout decays from L1 to L31.}
\label{tab:dense_routing_main}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
Layer & Trained readout & Untrained readout & Image\_mean \\
\midrule
L0 & 10.0\% & 10.0\% & 10.0\% \\
L1 & 100.0\% & 100.0\% & 10.0\% \\
L4 & 100.0\% & 99.1\% & 10.0\% \\
L8 & 100.0\% & 98.2\% & 10.0\% \\
L16 & 100.0\% & 92.3\% & 10.0\% \\
L31 & 100.0\% & 82.0\% & 10.0\% \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

%------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{VLA Fine-Tuning Front-Loads Instruction Routing}
\label{sec:learned_routing}

\paragraph{Three-level routing gradient at L1.}
We compare L1 readout attention across three conditions (Table~\ref{tab:routing_gradient}; Figure~\ref{fig:attention_profile}): untrained networks allocate 0/32 instruction-dominant heads ($11.7\%$ text); Llama-2 base 9/32 ($36.5\%$); VLA fine-tuning 24/32 ($57.7\%$), a redistribution beyond inherited priors.
Per-token normalization ($38.2\times$ text-to-image ratio; Appendix~\ref{sec:attention_decomp}) corroborates this redistribution.

\paragraph{Redistribution of routing across depth.}
The 32-layer profile (Figure~\ref{fig:attention_profile}) shows qualitatively distinct patterns.
Llama-2 base concentrates instruction attention at mid-depth (L9--L16: 15--22 heads).
VLA fine-tuning shifts to an entry-exit pattern (L0--L1: 21--24 heads, L31: 18), with intermediate layers markedly lower (0--11 at L2--L30).
Head counts reflect single-sample extraction; threshold sensitivity (30\%--70\%) analysis confirms ordering robustness across classification boundaries (Appendix~\ref{sec:threshold_sensitivity}).

\paragraph{Task-specific sharpening.}
A fourth condition (OpenVLA-7B fine-tuned on LIBERO) shows stable L1 entry routing (22/32 heads) but complete loss of L31 exit re-engagement (0 vs.\ 18 heads) and further mid-depth compression (L9--L16: 1--7 vs.\ 6--11), an $N=2$ comparison consistent with the hypothesis that exit routing serves multi-task flexibility, though training data diversity differences may also contribute.
Probe accuracy is preserved (L1: $99.7\%$; Appendix~\ref{app:finetuned_probe}).

\paragraph{Probe robustness corroborates the pattern.}
Cross-instruction probes corroborate the three-condition gradient (Appendix Figure~\ref{fig:probe_conditions_appendix}).
Training effects concentrate at the readout ($R^2$ $+0.069$, attention $+46$pp) while image\_mean changes minimally ($\Delta \leq 1.7\%$; Appendix~\ref{sec:training_effect_summary},~\ref{sec:action_r2_threecond}).

\paragraph{Open-loop functional sensitivity.}
We ablate attention to instruction tokens at the readout position.
Ablating all 24 dominant heads at L1 increases action $R^2$ by $+0.042$ (baseline $0.517$)\footnote{$R^2$ values differ from Table~\ref{tab:rsa_overview} due to the feature reconstruction pipeline; all comparisons are within-experiment.}.
We compare against 10 random subsets of 24 heads (Appendix~\ref{sec:random_subset_ablation}): random ablation yields a larger mean increase of $+0.077 \pm 0.013$ ($z = -2.73$, $p = 0.003$).
Dominant heads produce a smaller gain because they carry organized routing signals (structured noise for the probe); random heads lack such organization.
In the original extraction pipeline (\S\ref{sec:repr_extraction}), L1 ablation increases $R^2$ by $+0.059$ vs.\ no-ablation baseline, decaying at deeper layers (L8: $+0.018$; L13: $\Delta \approx 0$); instruction routing is an entry-layer phenomenon, consistent across all 10 tasks (Table~\ref{tab:per_task_r2}).

\paragraph{Action trajectory validation.}
Ablating 24 instruction-dominant heads alters $72.3\%$ of action tokens ($d = 0.65$); control ablation (8 heads) yields $d = 0.41$ ($29.3\%$), concentrated on the gripper dimension (shift $0.291$ vs.\ $< 0.034$; Tables~\ref{tab:attention_decomp},~\ref{tab:per_dim_mae}; Appendix~\ref{sec:action_trajectory}).

\paragraph{Dose-response gradient.}
Ablating 4--32 instruction-dominant heads yields monotonic divergence ($d = 0.482 \to 0.639$; Figure~\ref{fig:dose_response}), with steep rise through 12 heads and plateau beyond 20 (Appendix~\ref{sec:action_trajectory}).

\paragraph{Counterfactual instruction analysis.}
Replacing the original instruction with four perturbation types all produce substantial divergence: empty ($d = 0.968$), wrong task ($0.844$), paraphrased ($0.697$), shuffled ($0.625$; Table~\ref{tab:counterfactual}).
Token novelty ($1 - \text{Jaccard}$) predicts the ordering ($\rho = 1.0$): paraphrased (Jaccard $0.533$) exceeds shuffled (Jaccard $1.0$), though shuffled $d = 0.625$ with identical tokens reveals positional sensitivity.
L1 ablation ($d = 0.65$) falls within this range (Appendix~\ref{sec:action_trajectory}).

