\subsection{Non-Monotonic Refinement of Vision Encoder Output}
\label{sec:nonmonotonic}

\paragraph{Vision encoder provides geometric foundation.}
Table~\ref{tab:rsa_overview} reports RSA $\rho$ and probe $R^2$ between representations and actions.
DINOv2 \texttt{cls\_token} achieves the highest RSA ($\rho = 0.692$), exceeding the VLA peak ($0.628$ at L12) and CLIP ($0.557$); this ordering is metric-dependent (CKA reverses it; Appendix~\ref{sec:cka_appendix}).

\paragraph{Middle-layer peak (metric-dependent).}
Under RSA and action-probe decodability, visual representations show a middle-layer peak: trained image\_mean $\rho$ is stable through L12 ($0.628$), then declines to $0.422$ at L32 (Figure~\ref{fig:layer_curves}); probe $R^2$ peaks at L8--L10 ($0.655 \pm 0.002$; Appendix~\ref{sec:seed_sensitivity}).
The readout achieves $R^2 = 0.678$ (L13), exceeding the projector by $\Delta = 0.048$ (Table~\ref{tab:rsa_overview}).
Under linear CKA, this pattern disappears (monotonic increase; Appendix~\ref{sec:cka_appendix}); the non-monotonic trajectory characterizes rank-order geometry specifically, not global covariance structure.

\paragraph{Training primarily enhances readout decodability.}
Best-vs-best RSA gain is non-significant on LIBERO-Goal ($\Delta\rho = 0.017$, $3\%$, $N{=}10$ tasks; Appendix Table~\ref{tab:training_effects}), though LIBERO-Spatial yields a significant geometric gain ($\Delta = +0.023$, $p < 0.001$; Appendix~\ref{sec:cross_benchmark_appendix}).
The $N = 10$ instruction set limits statistical power for detecting small effects; non-significant differences should not be interpreted as evidence of absence.
